<section class="learning-loop" aria-labelledby="method-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">Why it works</p>
  <h2 id="method-title">Words that come to mind<br>when you speak and write.</h2>
  <p class="method-lede">Most vocabulary apps teach you to recognize words. WordLab combines five techniques so the words come to you on their own. Leave any one out and the words stay stuck in the app.</p>
  <div class="loop-grid">
    <article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Active recall</h3><p>You type the word from a sentence and its meaning. No multiple choice, so you learn to produce the word, not just recognize it.</p></article>
    <article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Spaced repetition</h3><p>Every learned word comes back just before you'd forget it, so your vocabulary keeps growing without more daily time.</p></article>
    <article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Speed-aware scheduling</h3><p>WordLab times each recall. Slow answers come back sooner until the word arrives quickly enough to use mid-sentence.</p></article>
    <article><span class="step-number">04</span><h3>Varied clues</h3><p>Each review uses a differently worded sentence, so the word connects to real situations, not one memorized prompt.</p></article>
    <article><span class="step-number">05</span><h3>Contrastive learning</h3><p>Drills separate close words like amiable and genial, or prudent and judicious, so you pick the right one. (Premium)</p></article>
    <article><span class="step-number">+</span><h3>Pictures and order</h3><p>Every word has an illustration of its meaning, and new words arrive roughly from most common to rarest.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p><a class="text-link" href="/how-it-works/">Read how the method works <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
</section>

